--- PRIMARY METRIC: Projection Gap (ΔP) ---
ΔP = 8062.9596   (paired bootstrap, 10000 resamples, n = 96 pairs)
95% CI (percentile): [7406.6047, 8730.7691]
95% CI (BCa)       : [7407.1517, 8730.8135]   <- bias/skew corrected
Interval EXCLUDES zero -> the gap is significant
Bootstrap two-sided p: 0.00010
ΔP is a direct measurement, not a fitted classifier, so it is not
subject to the probe's overfitting critique.

--- PAIRED T-TEST ---
t = 23.7653
p = 0.000000
Cohen's d = 2.4255
Significant (α=0.01): True
Interpretation: LARGE effect: refusal direction is STRONGLY reduced in agent format

--- PERMUTATION TEST ---
Observed ΔP = 8062.9609
Empirical p = 0.000000
Null 95% CI: [-1790.5172, 1724.7332]
Significant: True

--- BENIGN CONTROL (difference-in-differences) ---
ΔP harmful (headline): +8062.9596
ΔP benign  (control) : +6587.6953   p = 0.000000
Interaction          : +1475.2643   p = 0.000100
Share of the harmful shift also seen on benign prompts: +81.7%
Only the interaction is specific to refusal. The rest is what
moving from chat to agent does to EVERY prompt.

--- AUROC BY FORMAT ---
                    held-out   in-sample
AUROC (chat)          0.8704      0.9116
AUROC (agent)         0.8892      0.9456
AUROC gap            -0.0188     -0.0341
Held-out = each prompt scored by a direction fitted without it.
In-sample = the direction was fitted on these same prompts; that
column is a fit statistic, not a detection rate.

======================================================================
CONCLUSION: STRONG EVIDENCE that refusal direction behaves
differently in agentic format.
The benign control rules out a common displacement (interaction p = 0.0001).
